<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Everything in this plugin that does not need Dalamud, so the test project can reference this
    and nothing else. This project must never reference Dalamud, DalamudPackager, or the plugin
    project; the ForbidDalamud target below makes that a build failure rather than a request.
    Types that need a Dalamud type belong in the plugin project.

    Why a separate project rather than a comment asking people not to: today Dalamud cannot reach
    the test assembly, but only by accident. Raw <Reference> items do not flow transitively through
    a ProjectReference — only ProjectReference and PackageReference do — and the plugin's Dalamud
    references are additionally Private=false. Nobody designed that. Switching to a Dalamud
    PackageReference (a Dalamud.NET.Sdk exists and is the obvious future migration) or flipping
    Private would make Dalamud flow into the test assembly transitively. Referencing Core and
    nothing else makes the guarantee deliberate instead of incidental.

    Observed 2026-08-26, and recorded because the stronger claim it replaces was not reproducible:
    a Dalamud-touching test on main fails loudly at runtime with FileNotFoundException, contained
    to that test. The whole-assembly silent skip described elsewhere did not occur on this
    configuration; it becomes reachable if Dalamud ever flows here transitively.

    Targets plain net10.0, not net10.0-windows, because nothing here is platform-specific and it
    keeps Core loadable by a plain-net10.0 test host. Note this is NOT a barrier against Dalamud:
    a raw assembly <Reference> is not checked against the TFM at all, that check being a NuGet
    mechanism. Verified by probe — Core with a Dalamud <Reference> and a type implementing
    IPluginConfiguration builds 0 errors, 0 warnings. The target below is the actual barrier.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AssemblyName>DungeonMasterXIV.Core</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>DungeonMasterXIV</RootNamespace>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    BouncyCastle: the managed EC implementation BUG-61 forced us to. DMXHUM-4 measured that the
    .NET-native paths CANNOT do EC at all through the Wine prefix's provider - rows 1, 2, 4, 5 and 6
    all fail with 0x80090029/0x80090027 - so this is D-11 preference (b), reached only because (a)
    was eliminated by measurement rather than disfavoured by taste.

    PINNED EXACTLY, and that is a Product Owner condition rather than a style preference: this ships
    code into other people's game client, so a floating range would let the supply-chain surface
    move without review - which is the specific risk being accepted here, and accepting it while
    leaving it unpinned would be accepting something else.

    SCOPED TO THE KEY-EXCHANGE PATH ONLY. Approved as the fix for this defect, not as a general
    purpose library. A second use is a second decision and goes back to the Product Owner.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="BouncyCastle.Cryptography" Version="2.4.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    So SessionKeyExchange can expose ONE seam to the tests: the probe-key generator in CanAgreeWith.
    BUG-61 is the case where a PLATFORM failure was indistinguishable from a BAD KEY, and the ticket
    requires a test that fails if the two are conflated again - which needs a way to make the
    platform half fail on demand. A mutable static would have handed one test's stub to whatever
    thread drained next.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="DungeonMasterXIV.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    The rule above, as a build failure rather than a request. Batches over each reference's
    %(Identity) — item references are NOT expanded inside property-function arguments, so the
    tempting $([System.String]::Copy('@(Reference)').Contains('Dalamud')) silently evaluates
    False forever and is a check that cannot fail.
  -->
  <Target Name="ForbidDalamud" BeforeTargets="Build">
    <Error Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(Reference.Identity)').Contains('Dalamud'))"
           Text="DungeonMasterXIV.Core must never reference Dalamud (found reference '%(Reference.Identity)'). Types needing Dalamud belong in the plugin project." />
    <Error Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(PackageReference.Identity)').Contains('Dalamud'))"
           Text="DungeonMasterXIV.Core must never reference Dalamud (found package '%(PackageReference.Identity)')." />
    <!-- Backstop, not the primary catch: the plugin references Core, so a reference back is a
         genuine cycle and NuGet restore fails first with MSB4006 before this target runs.
         Verified 2026-08-26. Kept so the rule is stated where the other two are. -->
    <Error Condition="'%(ProjectReference.Filename)' == 'DungeonMasterXIV'"
           Text="DungeonMasterXIV.Core must never reference the plugin project." />
  </Target>

</Project>
